Recursive PTE layout regression
==============================

Build and run with updated machine headers on OpenBSD/amd64:

    make obj
    make regress

This unprivileged test checks every input byte, canonical recursive
addresses, recursive indexing through all four levels, and separation
from kernel VM, the kernel image, the early mapping, and all direct-map
placements.  It does not exercise the MMU or validate a running kernel.

Full runtime acceptance checklist

* Build and boot GENERIC, GENERIC.MP, and RAMDISK from the changed tree.
* Boot the same kernel image repeatedly, without KARL replacing it, and
  record pmap_pte_slot.  For endpoint test builds only, replace the seed
  load/mask in locore0.S with an immediate 0 or 127 before PTE_SLOT_MIN
  is added.  Changing the initial value of pmap_pte_rand in C does not
  force a slot: the loader overwrites .openbsd.randomdata.
* With kern.allowkmem enabled, build the sibling dump_tables utility
  against the updated headers.  Inspect -4, -4u, -4m, and -4um output.
  Normal kernel/process roots must have exactly one self-reference at
  the selected slot, pointing at their own physical root; slot 255 must
  be empty.  Meltdown user roots must have no recursive entry.  The -m
  variants require a CPU/configuration using that mitigation.
* Exercise fork/exec, copy-on-write, mmap/mprotect/munmap, and SMP page
  table reclamation with PCID enabled and disabled.  Measure shootdown
  cost: recursive addresses now use the existing upper-half invalidation
  path, including kernel, process, and temporary PCIDs on all CPUs.
* Test suspend/resume, hibernate, and crash-dump address translation.

